    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are equally recommended as class I first-line options for treating high blood pressure.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "ACE inhibitors",
            "link": "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.120.16667"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are equally recommended as class I first-line options for treating high blood pressure"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Differences"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "How they work: ACE inhibitors block your body from making angiotensin II (a hormone that narrows blood vessels). ARBs let the body make angiotensin II, but block the receptors it attaches to so blood vessels can stay relaxed.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "ACE inhibitors",
                "link": "https://www.kidney.org/kidney-topics/ace-inhibitors-and-arbs"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Naming convention: ACE inhibitors end in \"-pril\" (like lisinopril). ARBs end in \"-sartan\" (like losartan)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Effectiveness and Safety"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood pressure control: Both classes work equally well to lower blood pressure and protect your heart and kidneys."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side effects: ARBs have a better safety profile and are tolerated better than ACE inhibitors .",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "ARBs",
                "link": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8363588/"
              },
              {
                "text": "ACE inhibitors",
                "link": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6486121/"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cough and swelling: ACE inhibitors frequently cause a nagging dry cough (in up to 4% of people) and can rarely cause dangerous deep tissue swelling called angioedema. ARBs rarely cause a cough or angioedema, making them the preferred alternative if an ACE inhibitor causes side effects.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "ACE inhibitors",
                "link": "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2784663"
              },
              {
                "text": "ACE inhibitor",
                "link": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tto-zNH75JE"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like more tailored information, please let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you or a loved one currently experiencing side effects from a blood pressure medication (e.g., dry cough, dizziness)?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have any other health conditions (e.g., kidney disease, diabetes)?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you prepare questions to discuss with your doctor."
      }
    ],
